<p>North Korean leader Kim Jong-un signaled a conditional openness to improving relations with the United States, saying ties could improve if Washington acknowledges North Korea’s status as a nuclear-armed state. He framed the choice for the US as one between dialogue and confrontation, arguing that the future of the relationship depends on Washington’s attitude toward North Korea. At the same time, Kim shut the door on engagement with South Korea and paired that stance with blunt military threats, including warnings that North Korea could destroy the South if it feels its security is at risk. For global readers, the mixed message matters: it keeps a narrow diplomatic opening with the US alive while deepening inter-Korean tensions, raising the stakes for deterrence, communication channels, and crisis management on the Korean Peninsula.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/DeUZmQOsBbMJ-Nc77ZkX8LNzj3LH1WVM4L1h1iIyqPeyFw9SeG0_BedulNQ_7w7LQsWc6OYuq6nWTOMbVLppkT3wlpqUFEefUYdEAef_6luoSC8ElrGF4QZkOjt51gTduJDf0UfAz90cOpXtWC591tY3utCNkUQiMLz7ccEsdnRE1E_ZuAj4B47H' alt='A news broadcast in Seoul showing Kim Jong-un speaking at a Workers’ Party congress.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Status as settled reality: Kim presented North Korea’s nuclear-armed status as a settled fact—protected/enshrined at home—and said any improvement in ties starts with US recognition of that status.</li><li>End-hostility demand: Even as he spoke about getting along with the US, Kim urged Washington to end what Pyongyang calls its hostile policy toward North Korea.</li><li>Specific wording on Seoul: Kim said the North has “absolutely no business” with South Korea, underscoring Pyongyang’s line that it won’t pursue engagement even as Seoul promotes coexistence.</li><li>How the threat was framed: Kim tied the warning to “completely destroy” South Korea to a scenario in which North Korea judges its security to be under threat.</li><li>Binary-choice formulation: Kim cast the US decision as a choice between “peaceful coexistence” and “eternal confrontation,” saying the direction of ties depends “entirely” on Washington’s attitude.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>North Korean leadership (Kim Jong-un): Kim said North Korea could improve ties with the US if Washington acknowledges Pyongyang’s nuclear-armed status, and he argued that what happens next depends on the US approach. (<a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c0q3747jvnwo'>BBC News</a>)</li><li>North Korean leadership (Kim Jong-un): Kim said Pyongyang has no interest in dealing with South Korea and coupled that message with warnings that it would destroy the South if North Korea’s security is threatened. (<a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/north-korea-warns-destroy-south-threatened-leaves-door-130508617'>ABC News</a>)</li><li>South Korean government: South Korea’s president has tried to narrow gaps with the North by promoting peaceful coexistence—an approach Kim publicly rejected. (<a href='https://www.firstpost.com/world/we-have-no-dealings-with-our-enemy-north-koreas-kim-rejects-olive-branch-from-seoul-13983795.html'>Firstpost</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='http://www.euronews.com/2026/02/26/north-korea-shuns-relations-with-south-as-kim-jong-un-says-it-could-get-along-with-us'>North Korea shuns relations with South as Kim Jong-un says it could &#x27;get along&#x27; with US</a> - euronews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dw.com/en/north-korea-us-relations-depend-on-washington-s-attitude/a-76127360'>North Korea: US relations depend on Washington&#x27;s attitude</a> - dw.com</li><li><a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/north-korea-warns-destroy-south-threatened-leaves-door-130508617'>North Korea warns of destroying South if threatened, leaves door open for US dialogue</a> - abcnews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c0q3747jvnwo'>North Korea could &#x27;get along&#x27; with US, says Kim Jong Un</a> - bbc.com</li><li><a hr